Fair enough, it is minor. It isn't only visible with `print()` though, e.g.

```python
def limits(d):
    return max(int(d["low"]), int(d["high"]))
```

Compiled, `limits({})` raises `KeyError: 'high'` instead of `KeyError: 'low'`, because `d["high"]` is evaluated first. The fix doesn't add any temps or runtime cost, it only changes the order of the assignments in the generated C.
